.TH SCENARIO 1 "2026-09-20" "caveutils" "caveutils Manual"
.SH NAME
scenario \- real keyboard/mouse macro record+play - tries multiple real backends
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B scenario record [NAME] [DEVICE...]
.br
.B scenario stop
.br
.B scenario play NAME
.br
.B scenario list
.br
.B scenario delete NAME
.br
.B scenario rename OLD NEW
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B scenario
.nf
so this works across distros without hardcoding one specific tool:
evemu (kernel evdev level - real, portable across BOTH X11 and
Wayland since it never touches the display server at all, confirmed
installed on this machine) first, falling back to xnee/cnee (X11-
only, XTEST-based - a real, well-known tool for exactly this, but
not packaged for this machine's own distro, so its branch below is
real but UNVERIFIED - stated honestly, not tested against a real
install anywhere).

The real source this project used to have for this (fezscenario,
from the user's own ~/.mydot/toolbox) is unrecoverable - every
symlink to it is broken, no git history, no filesystem snapshot
exists despite the btrfs @snapshots subvolume being there (checked
by hand: mounted, empty). The only real trace left is a single
stale PID in ~/.local/share/fezscenario/.rec_pids - which is what
confirms the real design choice below (one recorder process PER
device, multiple PIDs tracked at once for a keyboard+mouse pair)
rather than being a guess.

usage: scenario record [NAME] [DEVICE...]
         starts one real recorder per DEVICE (default: every real
         keyboard+mouse device found in /proc/bus/input/devices)
       scenario stop
         stops the recorder(s) started by the last `record`
       scenario play NAME
       scenario list
       scenario delete NAME | rename OLD NEW
       scenario                    (no arguments: the TUI below)

The TUI (lib/lazytui.sh) lists the recordings with a live "recording"
marker and refreshes itself; Enter plays, Ctrl-R records a new one,
Ctrl-X stops recording, Ctrl-D deletes, Alt-N renames. Playing waits
PLAY_DELAY seconds first (top of the script, or SCENARIO_DELAY) so you can
put the focus where the input should go; 0 plays at once.

NEEDS ROOT for the evemu backend (reading/writing real
/dev/input/eventN nodes - confirmed by hand: evemu-record can't even
list devices as this project's own non-root user).

NOT live-tested against real hardware here, deliberately: actually
running `scenario play` injects real synthetic keyboard/mouse events
into whatever currently has focus - doing that from an unattended
agent session risks hijacking the user's own live input. Only
syntax (`sh -n`) and the device-detection parsing (checked against
this machine's own real /proc/bus/input/devices) were verified.

.fi
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B record [NAME] [DEVICE...]
record keyboard and mouse
.TP
.B stop
stop the recording
.TP
.B play NAME
replay a recording (after PLAY_DELAY seconds)
.TP
.B list
list recordings
.TP
.B delete NAME
remove a recording
.TP
.B rename OLD NEW
rename a recording
